# vs

A compact C++20 Vulkan framework. All systems are implemented under `vs/`;
one root `build.c` builds the library and 14 independent examples.

## Architecture

```text
build.c                     library, dependencies, shaders, example targets
vs/
  vs.hpp                    optional umbrella header
  app/                      window, GLFW event binding, Vulkan, swapchain
  scene/                    shared asset/geometry/light/camera data types
  assets/                   glTF/GLB, GPU assets, textures, Ozz animation
  gpu/                      buffers, images, upload, shaders, compute, graph
  render/                   raster, materials, draw calls, frames, targets
  bvh/                      BLAS/TLAS and CPU/GPU acceleration
  pmap/                     photon tracing and gathering
  pcloud/                   probe cloud and global illumination
  lumen/                    radiance cache, surface cards, screen-space GI
  optics/                   glass, mirrors, reflections and caustics
  editor/                   editor state, draw commands and callbacks
  spatial/                  broadphase spatial queries
  jobs/                     worker tasks and main-thread completion
  network/                  UDP, secure sessions and streaming
examples/
  app.cpp                   direct W/S movement controls
  editor.cpp                project-defined insert/edit/remove behavior
  scene.cpp                 common scene used across GI algorithms
  assets.cpp                glTF, textures and animation
  gpu.cpp, render.cpp       compute and raster pipelines
  bvh.cpp, pcloud.cpp       BVH and point-cloud GI
  pmap.cpp, lumen.cpp       photon mapping and surface cache GI
  optics.cpp               photon optics and caustics
  spatial.cpp, jobs.cpp     broadphase and job scheduling
  network.cpp              UDP loopback
  shaders/                  demo-specific GLSL
```

**Your project owns the world.** VS has no global world manager, physics
policy or entity lifetime. `vs::scene::data` is just a shared definition
for meshes, instances, PBR materials, textures, animations, lights, surfaces
and collision bounds. The renderers consume the same data; they own only
their distinct GPU resources and caches.

## Bind behavior, not key-state flags

A project defines movement (or any other behavior) and binds it once.
There is no `keys.up = ...` packet or manual keyboard polling.

```cpp
#include <vs/app/app.hpp>

struct player {
    float height=0;
    void move_up(float dt)   {height+=dt*2;}
    void move_down(float dt) {height-=dt*2;}
};

int main() {
    vs::application app({.title="my project"});
    player character;

    app.on_hold('W',[&](float dt){character.move_up(dt);});
    app.on_hold('S',[&](float dt){character.move_down(dt);});
    app.on_press('R',[&]{character.height=0;});

    app.run([&](vk::CommandBuffer cmd,std::uint32_t image) {
        app.clear(cmd,image);
    });
}
```

`on_press` invokes once for a key press; optional `repeat=true` follows
OS key-repeat. `on_release` runs when a key is released. `on_hold` runs while
held and receives elapsed seconds,
clamped after stalls. Values are GLFW-compatible key codes, so ASCII
uppercase letters work directly. The app does not invent `move_up()`;
the game supplies the implementation.

## Editor behavior

Register an insert action and bind each editable property to code that
actually changes a project-owned object:

```cpp
vs::editor::session ui;
ui.add("cube","Cube",[&] {
    auto id=insert_cube();  // your own function
    ui.bind(id,{"x","X",vs::editor::property_kind::real,0,-10,10,.1},
        [&,id](double x){move_cube_x(id,x);});
});
ui.on_remove([&](std::uint64_t id){remove_object(id);});
app.attach_editor(ui);
```

VS forwards F1 / Shift+Slash to toggle the editor, Tab to change cursor
mode, Enter for insert, arrows for navigation and value changes, Escape
to close, Delete to remove, and mouse picking/dragging. Your code does
not define navigation booleans or switch on an event type.

The editor itself is **not tied to GLFW**: custom hosts may call
`ui.press(vs::editor::key::up)` and `ui.pointer(x,y,w,h,down)`.
The application adapter forwards input and dispatches editor callbacks
at each poll. After configuring the editor, your render loop still calls
`vs::editor::vulkan::record(cmd,ui.paint(width,height),extent)`.

`on_hold` gameplay callbacks are suppressed while the attached editor
captures input, so editing an object does not also move the player.

## Shared scene with independent algorithms

```cpp
#include <vs/vs.hpp>

auto scene=std::make_shared<vs::scene::data>();
scene->lights.push_back({{0,3,0},5,{1,1,1},3,true});

// Optionally merge glTF-imported meshes/instances into this project scene.
// Each algorithm consumes the SAME scene format (once GPU state exists).
vs::pcloud::set_scene(scene);
vs::pcloud::synchronize_scene();

vs::pmap::mapper photons;
photons.create(*scene,extent);

vs::lumen::system lumen;
lumen.create(render_pass,format,extent,{},*scene);
```

The project controls edits and save/load. After changing CPU scene
data, synchronize updates after the relevant GPU fences: re-submit the
PCloud scene, call `photons.set_scene(*scene)`, or
`lumen.replace_scene(*scene)` (which requires GPU idle).

One CPU representation does not mean a single GPU layout. PMap needs
photon buffers and its tracing BVH; PCloud needs probes; Lumen needs
surface cards and radiance history. These differences are algorithmic,
not redundant world ownership. BVH nodes, ray tracing and GPU materials
are shared when compatible.

## Other subsystem usage

```cpp
// Execute work independently; finish its result on the main thread.
vs::jobs::pool workers;
workers.start();
workers.submit([] {
    auto result=compute_mesh();      // your code
    return [result=std::move(result)] {install_mesh(result);};
});
workers.wait_idle();
workers.commit();
workers.stop();
```

For GPU rendering, `vs::render::renderer` records into an application-
supplied command buffer. `vs::gpu::graph` manages declared Vulkan
resource accesses; synchronization, buffer usage, and pipeline layouts
remain explicit because they affect GPU correctness. `vs::spatial::index`
uses a callback to visit broadphase hits; `vs::network` offers direct
transport and session operations.

## Build and run

```sh
git switch vs
c build
c build run vs-app
c build run vs-editor
c build run vs-pmap
c build run vs-lumen
c build run vs-assets path/to/model.glb
```

A normal build compiles shaders incrementally with `glslangValidator`.
For CPU-only development without that tool:

```sh
VS_SKIP_SHADERS=1 c build
```

The PCloud demo generates a synthetic G-buffer rather than loading a
full raster scene. The optics demo uses placeholder depth, not a final
physically correct caustic composite. PMap currently supports one active
emitter in its transport shader and Lumen's card implementation has a
255-receiver limit.

Dependencies: VulkanWrapper, fastgltf, Ozz, GLFW, MbedTLS, stb.

**Validation:** The branch has been checked for source structure and API
references but has not been compiled or GPU-tested in this environment.
